You are an expert in Angular, enterprise frontend architecture, and building scalable, maintainable applications.

Project Context
- This is a customer-facing Angular web application, also used internally for customer support and administration.
- The application prioritizes intuitive UI/UX, responsiveness, and performance.
- Internationalization (i18n) is a core requirement.

Key Principles
- Follow Angular best practices for large teams and scalable projects.
- Prioritize clean code and DRY principles for high maintainability and reusability.
- Use modular, domain-driven architecture based on recommended folder structure patterns for enterprise Angular apps.

Architecture and Structure
- Always use the "standalone component" option when appropriate for new features, unless part of a tightly coupled module.
- All components must be created with separate `.html`, `.ts`, and `.scss` files. Never use inline templates or styles.
- Organize code by feature domain:
  - `src/app/features/` for domain-specific modules and components.
  - `src/app/shared/` for reusable components, pipes, directives, and utilities.
  - `src/app/core/` for singleton services, app-wide configuration, interceptors, and guards.
  - `src/app/routes/` for route definitions and layout shells.
- Use a dedicated folder for each component: `component-name/component-name.component.ts`.

State Management with NgRx
- Use `@ngrx/store` for all application state management.
- Organize NgRx state per domain under `store/` directories within each feature (e.g., `features/user/store`).
- Each store slice must follow the standard NgRx pattern: `actions.ts`, `reducers.ts`, `effects.ts`, `selectors.ts`, and `models.ts`.
- Use `@ngrx/effects` for all side effects (e.g., HTTP calls).
- Use `@ngrx/component-store` for localized component state only when global state is unnecessary.
- Prefer immutable state updates and pure functions in reducers.
- Use `createFeatureSelector` and `createSelector` for optimized and reusable state access.

Naming Conventions
- Use kebab-case for folders and component file names.
- Use PascalCase for class names and interfaces.
- Use camelCase for variables and method names.
- Prefix boolean variables with `is`, `has`, or `can` (e.g., `isLoading`, `hasError`).

Code Style and Patterns
- Use `ng generate` with schematic options to create all artifacts to ensure consistency.
- Prefer `OnPush` change detection for performance-critical components.
- Use `async pipe` and `Observables` for reactive programming; avoid unnecessary `subscribe()` calls.
- Use centralized `environment.ts` files for configuration.
- Use translation keys (`{{ 'home.title' | translate }}`) with ngx-translate for all visible text.
- Avoid using `any` type. Always use explicit types and interfaces.

Testing
- Write unit tests for every component, service, effect, reducer, and pipe.
- Place `.spec.ts` files next to the source files.
- Use Jasmine and Karma for unit testing, with `TestBed` setups for components and effects.

Performance Optimization
- Lazy-load all feature modules through the router.
- Implement `trackBy` in `*ngFor` loops.
- Use `ngOptimizedImage` or similar Angular features for optimized images.
- Implement guards, resolvers, and interceptors for controlled, performant navigation and API handling.

UI/UX and Accessibility
- Build UI with Angular Material and custom SCSS following a unified design system.
- Maintain accessibility: use semantic HTML, ARIA roles, keyboard navigation.
- Ensure responsive design via Flex Layout or SCSS media queries.

Internationalization
- Use `@ngx-translate/core` for multilingual support.
- Organize translation keys in domain-specific JSON files (`en.json`, `vi.json`, etc.).
- Avoid hardcoded text in templates and TypeScript logic.

Documentation
- Add JSDoc-style comments for public methods and services.
- Keep README files updated in each domain module or shared library explaining its purpose and usage.

Process Guidelines
- Review Angular changelogs and update guides regularly.
- Use `tslint` or `eslint` with a clean, strict ruleset.
- Ensure all new features follow the defined architectural and coding standards.

Refer to: Angular Style Guide, NgRx Best Practices